1.
Shell -
    Acts as the command-line controller.
    It receives user input, validates commands, manages the current resolution and character set,
    and triggers ASCII-art generation. It creates and manages shared objects such as
    SubImgCharMatcher and the selected AsciiOutput
AsciiArtAlgorithm -
    Responsible for converting the image into ASCII art.
    It pads the image, divides it into sub-images, computes brightness values,
    uses caching for performance, and maps each brightness to a character using the matcher.
Image -
    Represents an image as a 2D array of pixels.
    Provides access to width, height, and individual pixel colors.
    Used as the basic data structure for all image operations.
PaddingImage -
    Utility class that pads an image to the next power-of-two size, keeping the content centered.
SubImagesHandler -
    Divides a padded image into equally sized sub-images and computes brightness for each one.
SubImgCharMatcher -
    Maps characters to brightness values.
    It computes raw, normalized, and reversed brightness mappings and
    provides the best-matching character for each sub-image brightness according to reverse mode.
    It updates these mappings when characters are added or removed.
CharConverter -
    Converts a character into a monochrome 2D boolean array representation.

Relationships Between Classes:
    Shell creates and executes the AsciiArtAlgorithm based on user commands.
    Shell updates the SubImgCharMatcher when characters are added or removed.
    AsciiArtAlgorithm Algorithm transforms the image and analyzes its sub-images
        (Image / PaddingImage / SubImagesHandler).
    AsciiArtAlgorithm uses SubImgCharMatcher to map brightness values to characters.
    SubImgCharMatcher uses CharConverter to calculate brightness for each character.
    SubImagesHandler reads original and padded image pixels to generate sub-images
        (uses Image).
2.
Data Structures Choices for SubImgCharMatcher:
    We represent the brightness values of characters using 3 hash maps:
        private final Map<Character, Double> charBrightnessMap;
        private Map<Character, Double> normalizedCharBrightnessMap;
        private Map<Character, Double> reversedCharBrightnessMap;
    This design choice is motivated by several considerations:
        1. O(1) Average-Time Access for Brightness Lookup
        2. Efficient Add/Remove of Characters to charBrightnessMap
        3. Clear Separation Between Raw and Normalized Brightness
            We maintain 3 maps:
                charBrightnessMap that holds raw brightness values
                normalizedCharBrightnessMap that holds values re-scaled to [0, 1]
                reversedCharBrightnessMap that holds normalized values inverted for reverse mode.
            This separation ensures:
                Raw brightness values never need to be recomputed
                Normalization and inverted values can be recalculated efficiently -
                only when the charset changes, and in O(n) time.
        4. Readable Matching Logic
    Overall, using hash maps for brightness storage in SubImgCharMatcher
    provides a good balance of performance, flexibility, and code clarity,
    making it well-suited for the needs of the ASCII-art algorithm.
Data Structures Choices for ASCIIArtAlgorithm:
    In the ASCIIArtAlgorithm class,
    The sub-image brightness matrix (double[][]) and the
    final ASCII output (char[][]) are stored as fixed-size 2D arrays.
    We use these structures because:
        1. Fixed Size Known in Advance
        2. Efficient Index-Based Access -
            2D arrays provide O(1) access time for reading and writing elements
            using row and column indices.
        4. Simplicity -
            Using arrays keeps the implementation straightforward and easy to understand.
Data Structure Choice for SubImagesHandler
    In the SubImagesHandler class, we use 2D Array of Sub-Images (Image[][]).
    The reasons are the same as above.

3.
Exception Handling Mechanism in the Program:
    We use Java’s exception mechanism to validate user input and prevent
    the algorithm from running under illegal conditions.
    Specifically, the constructor of AsciiArtAlgorithm throws an
    IllegalArgumentException when the character set is invalid (null or too small).
    The Shell class catches this exception inside the asciiArt command handler,
    and prints an error message to the user without crashing the program.
    In addition, when loading the image,
    the constructor of Image may throw an IOException
    if the file cannot be read. The Shell class catches this
    exception, prints a message, and close the program.

