I didn't add changes in the code of the given classes.

6. Additional notes:
To improve the performance of the ASCII-art algorithm,
we implemented a caching mechanism that avoid redundant
computations across multiple runs of the algorithm:
    Caching Sub-Image Brightness Values -
        if the image and resolution remain unchanged between runs,
        the brightness values will remain the same.
        So, to avoid recomputing brightness values on subsequent runs, we store:
            double[][] prevBrightness;
            int prevResolution;
            Image prevImage;
        if all three match the current run, the algorithm reuses the cached brightness matrix
        instead of recalculating it.
We also improve performance by computing the SubImgCharMatcher once in the Shell
instead of rebuilding it inside every execution of the ASCII-art algorithm.
The matcher depends only on the character set, not on the image or resolution,
so there is no need to recompute brightness values and normalization on each run.
The Shell updates the matcher only when the charset changes or when reverse mode is activated.
Otherwise, it simply reuses the existing matcher.
This avoids redundant work, reduces runtime across repeated outputs,
and keeps the algorithm itself focused solely on converting brightness values to ASCII characters.

